  #line-at(85.544pt, 581.384pt, "As for the modelling, we utilized OEMOF (Open Energy Modelling Framework), which ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 597.224pt, "is an open-source tool based on Python, created for simulating and optimizing intricate ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 613.064pt, "energy systems. This tool allows for the creation of mixed linear optimization (MPLI) ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 628.904pt, "problems by illustrating system elements, like solar energy generation units, consumption ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 644.984pt, "patterns, grid import and export, as well as storage; seen as nodes in a graph framework. ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 660.824pt, "In this study, OEMOF played a crucial role in analysing both the energy balance as well ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 676.664pt, "as the techno-economic performance under two different scenarios: standalone facilities ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 692.504pt, "and a combined facility. It is important to note that OEMOF itself is not a solver; rather, ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 708.344pt, "it requires integration with an external optimization solver. For this purpose, we ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 724.184pt, "employed Gurobi academic license. ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 754.184pt, "The system was represented as a straightforward pipe labeled \"electricity,\" into which ")
  #line-at(85.544pt, 770.024pt, "energy from a source (the solar power plant) flows, while the sinks (the users) are directly ")
]

